# The Scream: who is screaming?
subtitle: "The figure in Munch's Scream isn't screaming: it is hearing one, and every line of the painting is bent to carry that sound."
tags: ["the scream","edvard munch","painting","expressionism","art history","norway"]
teaches: ["concepts/expressionism","skills/reading-a-painting"]
thumb: "band \"He's not the one screaming\" picture \"File:Edvard Munch, 1893, The Scream, oil, tempera and pastel on cardboard, 91 x 73 cm, National Gallery of Norway.jpg\" colour bottom"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly fascinated"}

## The Scream: who is screaming?
sources:
    - id: nm
      title: The Scream (Skrik), 1893
      authors: Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Tempera and crayon on cardboard, 1893. Munch''s notebook, 22 January 1892: walking with two friends, the sky turned blood-red, his friends walked on, and he felt a great, unending scream through nature.'
      url: https://www.nasjonalmuseet.no/en/guide/collection/60/the-scream/
    - id: prata
      title: Sky in Edvard Munch's The Scream
      authors: Prata, Robock and Hamblyn
      year: 2018
      finding: The sky's colours and waves fit nacreous (mother-of-pearl) clouds better than the older Krakatoa-ash explanation; both remain hypotheses.

    card title "The Scream"

Why does The Scream look the way it does: a melting face, a sky on fire, a whole world bending?
    image art cites ["nm"] credit "Edvard Munch, 1893. Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo. Public domain (Wikimedia Commons)." x 290 height 620 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Edvard_Munch%2C_1893%2C_The_Scream%2C_oil%2C_tempera_and_pastel_on_cardboard%2C_91_x_73_cm%2C_National_Gallery_of_Norway.jpg/1920px-Edvard_Munch%2C_1893%2C_The_Scream%2C_oil%2C_tempera_and_pastel_on_cardboard%2C_91_x_73_cm%2C_National_Gallery_of_Norway.jpg look screen regions.bands [0.4,0.1,0.45,0.15] regions.boats [0.29,0.345,0.11,0.03] regions.face [0.46,0.49,0.14,0.15] regions.figure [0.39,0.485,0.25,0.515] regions.fjord [0.12,0.29,0.82,0.17] regions.friends [0.01,0.34,0.12,0.2] regions.hands [0.42,0.55,0.21,0.12] regions.head [0.4,0.47,0.24,0.22] regions.railing [0.12,0.43,0.33,0.22] regions.red_sky [0,0.16,0.5,0.12] regions.road_end [0,0.36,0.14,0.12] regions.signature [0.03,0.958,0.15,0.035] regions.sky [0,0,0.94,0.3] regions.swirl [0.45,0.31,0.45,0.14]

It was painted in 1893 by Edvard Munch, a Norwegian who wanted pictures of people who breathe and feel and suffer, not quiet rooms.
    highlight art:signature effect magnify

Most people take it as a man screaming in terror.
    highlight art:face effect magnify

But look at his hands: they are pressed over his ears.
    camera on ["art:head"] duration 2.5

That's what you do when a noise comes at you, not when you make one.
    highlight art:hands strength strong

    camera reset true duration 1.5

Munch wrote down what happened: he was walking with two friends at sunset, on a road above the fjord at Oslo.
    highlight art:friends effect magnify

Suddenly, he wrote, the sky turned blood red, and he stood there trembling with anxiety.
    camera on ["art:sky"] duration 3

    camera reset true duration 1.2

And he felt what he called an endless scream passing through nature.
    highlight art:sky art:fjord effect light rest dim

He first showed the painting as The Scream of Nature. So the scream belongs to the world, and the painting has to make you hear it.
    highlight art:sky art:fjord

That's why the sky isn't painted as clouds, but as long curving bands, like ripples of sound.
    highlight art:bands effect detail

The same waves run down into the fjord, which swirls instead of lying flat.
    highlight art:swirl effect detail

Then they pass through the man himself: his body sways with the very same curves.
    camera on ["art:figure"] duration 3

    camera reset true duration 1.5

Even his face has worn down to a skull: the eyes and mouth are just dark holes.
    highlight art:face effect detail

Against all those curves, one thing is ruler-straight: the railing, rushing away at a steep angle.
    highlight art:railing effect detail

It drags your eye down the road so fast that the ground seems to tilt.
    arrow eye_path #f2c14e from.x 264 from.y 277 to.x 103 to.y 416 style.stroke_width 7

    erase eye_path

And at its far end, the two friends stay upright and calm. They hear nothing.
    highlight art:friends effect detail

So the strange look has a logic: a straight, calm world, and one person caught in a bending one.
    highlight art:figure effect light strength spotlight rest dim lift true

As for the red sky, scientists have looked for a real cause, from volcanic ash to rare glowing clouds; but Munch was painting a feeling, not the weather.
    highlight art:red_sky effect detail

    quiz "A painter wants to show someone overwhelmed by the din of a city, the way Munch did. Which picture follows his method?"
        right: The bent streets: the noise is painted into the world around one person, and the others stay calm and straight.
        wrong: Munch put the feeling into the world around the person, and kept everyone else calm.
        * A realistic street, one person's mouth wide open
        + Streets bent into waves that shake one person; others walk on upright
        * A crowd all screaming under a calm, ordinary sky
        * A close-up of one ear, in hot reds and yellows

So next time you see it, don't ask why he's screaming. Ask what he's hearing.
    camera on ["art:head"] duration 4
---
